ry  spokeswoman  here uh in moscow in her weekly uh  meeting weekly audience with the press  she said uh that what  the sanctions the u.s have announced do  not meet the interests  of two nuclear powers the actions show  the opposite tone to the phone call to  the phone call made  between president biden who initiated  that phone call on tuesday night  and uh vladimir putin she says  that the sanctions are basis in  substance and senseless  in form and our response is inevitable  it's coming she says  the u.s ambassador's already been  summoned to the foreign ministry he's  apparently there they say  and this foreign minister spokeswoman  says he won't be getting  an easy ride but the issue is that the  us has a  long list of complaints that you heard  roslyn refer to there  against uh russia about solarwinds hack  that targeted nine federal  uh organizations in the us plus 100  companies the biggest  and most sophisticated cyber attack the  world's ever seen according to microsoft  it refers to the occupation continued  occupation of crimea  and the repression in crimea and the  jailing of alexander navalny so  all of that long list of complaints is  why the u.s says it's now taken this  action  so what if any economic impacts are  likely from  these new sanctions bernard  well one interesting indicator is that  before the detail of these  sanctions were announced the russian  ruble dropped  in value against the dollar but after  the sanctions were announced the ruble  regained what it had lost against the  dollar and this is because although  the us is stopping the buying of russian  debt  on the foot on the primary bonds market  you'll still be able to buy it  on the secondary market that means that  someone else who buy another organ  another country  or another organization that buys that  debt russia will still be able to buy it  people will still be able to buy russian  debt on the secondary market so  the economic effect is not as  significant as the markets